-## Scene 1 — What can we delegate?
-<!-- claim_id: 01-c6 -->
+### Scene 1 — What can we delegate?
+<!-- 01-c7 -->
 
 **Narration**
 
-Which research tasks can we delegate to agents, and what makes their results trustworthy? For students, the useful development in this reporting window is a richer record of bounded experimentation: matched comparisons, explicit budgets, recorded failures, and human selection gates. Our interpretation is that these reports make delegation more testable. They do not establish general autonomous discovery. The question is whether the whole experimental loop deserves your trust.
+Which research tasks can you delegate to an agent, and what makes its results trustworthy? This is Research Loop, as of October fifteenth, twenty twenty-six. The September sixteenth to October fifteenth evidence window adds a more concrete account of bounded experimental loops. Our interpretation is practical: delegate a defined change, keep evaluation fixed, and decide in advance what evidence would satisfy the scientific claim. These reports support testing specific workflows. They do not establish that autonomous scientific discovery is solved.
 
 **Visual**
 
-Title and exact as-of date. Draw: **Propose → Run → Evaluate → Record → Human gate**. Label the takeaway **Interpretation**.
+Title and exact as-of date. Animate a simple diagram: “Defined change → Experiment → Fixed evaluation → Scientific acceptance check.” Label it **Interpretation**, with citations to [solar segmentation, §2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10491) and [ResearchFlow-Eval, Appendix G](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18088). Excerpt locator: “a reported success can refer to a narrower experiment than the required claim.”
 
-Source locators: [Malena](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40303), §4.4, “The base Malena iteration performs no significantly worse than any intervention tested.” [Agora](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18094), Abstract, “Measuring the effect on discovery per unit of compute requires a matched comparison.”
