-## Scene 2 — Compare under matched conditions
-<!-- claim_id: 01-c1 -->
+### Scene 2 — A gain with a human gate
+<!-- 01-c1 -->
 
 **Narration**
 
-Start with Malena. Under matched compute resources, time budgets, and backbone models, the study reports a medal rate of 62.5 percent with GLM 5.2, compared with 47.1 percent for its best external harness comparison. That is a benchmark-specific result. It is evidence about the tested setup, not a guarantee for your project. The authors also identify possible benchmark contamination, limited statistical power, and possible differences in tuning effort as limitations.
+First, NanoProteinLM. Its authors report long-range contact precision rising from twenty-eight point two to thirty-six point six percent, measured as P at L, at the same forty-eight point four billion token budget. Humans decided which recipes to scale up. That human gate is part of the result, not a detail to remove from the headline. This is a bounded, author-reported improvement, rather than evidence of fully autonomous discovery.
 
 **Visual**
 
-Two bars on a zero-based percentage axis: **Malena: 62.5%** and **Best external harness tested: 47.1%**. Caption: **Author-reported · GLM 5.2 · benchmark-specific**.
+Two bars on the same zero-based scale: “28.2%” and “36.6% P@L.” Persistent labels: **Author-reported**, “Same 48.4B-token budget,” and “Human scaling decisions.” [Source](https://jojoml.github.io/blog/2026/nanoproteinlm-agenticls/), section “A better PLM recipe by hill-climbing NanoProteinLM”; excerpt locator: “with a human deciding which recipes to scale up”.
 
-[Source: Malena](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40303). Locators: §5, excerpt beginning “Crucially, all methods are evaluated”; concluding excerpt beginning “With GLM 5.2, Malena earns a medal”; **Limitations**. Display the matching-conditions excerpt alongside the chart.
