-## Scene 3 — Keep the scientific goal visible
-<!-- claim_id: 01-c2 -->
+### Scene 3 — Search needs confirmation
+<!-- 01-c2 -->
 
 **Narration**
 
-NanoProteinLM connects search to a scientific proxy, with a person choosing which recipes to scale. Its authors report long-range contact precision increasing from 28.2 to 36.6 percent at the same 48.4 billion-token training budget. Contact precision is a proxy for downstream protein folding. Equal final training tokens do not establish equal total search costs. Keep both qualifications attached to the result: a human selected scale-up candidates, and the reported training budget does not settle the cost of finding them.
+The same project also shows why confirmation matters. Four of seven proposed changes survived the final ablation study. Three did not remain in the final set. The practical interpretation is that a promising search result still needs a separate confirmation stage. Finding a candidate and confirming that its contribution survives further testing are different steps. Preserve that distinction when you decide what an agent has actually established.
 
 **Visual**
 
-Diagram: **Sequential search → Human selection → Scale-up**. Below: **Contact precision: 28.2% → 36.6% P@L**. Label **Authors’ report**.
+Seven small boxes flow through “Final ablation”; four remain highlighted. Label the count **Source claim** and the confirmation lesson **Interpretation**. [Source](https://jojoml.github.io/blog/2026/nanoproteinlm-agenticls/), “What survived the human gate”; exact excerpt: “Four of the seven changes survive the final ablation study.”
 
-[Source: NanoProteinLM authors’ blog](https://jojoml.github.io/blog/2026/nanoproteinlm-agenticls/). Locators: **A better PLM recipe by hill-climbing NanoProteinLM**, excerpt beginning “Starting from the ESMC recipe”; **Failure mode: Perfect on reward, failure on science**, first paragraph. Display: “with a human deciding which recipes to scale up”.
