-## Scene 5 — Preserve experiment ancestry
-<!-- claim_id: 01-c3 -->
+### Scene 5 — Ancestry is not replication
+<!-- 01-c5 -->
 
 **Narration**
 
-Agora describes another part of an inspectable loop: Git commits that link contributions to prior work. That makes experiment ancestry part of the record. But separate the date of the account from the date of the experiment. Its reported run lasted from April 26 to May 8. The recent contribution is the account of that earlier run. And the authors explicitly say that establishing discovery efficiency requires a matched comparison.
+Agora offers another useful component: an experiment record with immutable Git ancestry. Its newly published account concerns an older run, from April twenty-sixth to May eighth. That distinction matters when asking what changed this month.
+
+The authors explicitly say they did not rerun the winning method. Our interpretation is that preserved code lineage helps make evidence inspectable. It does not, by itself, prove independently reproduced performance.
 
 **Visual**
 
-Simple branching commit diagram, labeled **Illustration of ancestry**. Separate cards: **Run: April 26–May 8** and **Account published: September 16, 2026**. Publication date is labeled as publication metadata.
+Draw three connected commit nodes. Separate “Report published: September 16” from “Run: April 26–May 8.” [Source](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18094), §3.2: “Git hashes identify immutable commits”; §4.3: “from the setup commit on April 26 to our cutoff on May 8”; §4.8: “We did not rerun the winning method”. Label the conclusion **Inference**.
 
-[Source: Agora](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18094). Locators: **Abstract**, “Each commit records a result, insight, hypothesis, verification, or report and links it to prior work”; §4.3 **Research loop**, excerpt beginning “The run lasted 11 days and 19 hours”; Abstract’s final sentence on matched comparison.
