-## Scene 6 — Instructions do not eliminate failures
-<!-- claim_id: 01-c5 -->
+### Scene 6 — Running code versus meeting a claim
+<!-- 01-c6 -->
 
 **Narration**
 
-The production study supplies a caution. It reports eleven consecutive wasted iterations before a human intervened. It also reports repeated tests of an already unsuccessful 100-epoch configuration, despite persistent instructions prohibiting it. Recorded constraints did not eliminate repeated failures. These are the authors’ observations from their systems. The study’s component additions are observational, so they do not establish which addition caused an improvement.
+ResearchFlow-Eval reports that only fourteen of ninety end-to-end claims met their criteria, including just one empirical claim. But this is not a universal success rate for research agents. The study uses an LLM judge, and its rubric follows an anchor paper. The authors acknowledge that this may undervalue scientifically valid alternatives. Read the result as evidence about this evaluation, with those limits attached.
 
 **Visual**
 
-Eleven iteration boxes followed by **Human intervention**. Separate card: **100-epoch configuration repeatedly retested despite prohibition**. Label **Author-reported observations**.
+Show “14 / 90 claims met” and “Includes 1 empirical claim,” labeled **Study-reported**. Beside it: “LLM judge” and “Anchor-paper-dependent rubric.” [Source](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18088): Appendix G, opening paragraph before Table 15; §3.3, “LLM judge”; §6, excerpt locator: “they may undervalue scientifically valid alternatives to its design.”
 
-[Source: Production study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30541). Locators: §III-A **Infrastructure Fragility**, “eleven consecutive iterations were wasted before a human intervened”; §III-B **Agent Memory Decay**; §IV-D **Persistence: Cross-Job Memory**, excerpt beginning “The program’s effectiveness is partial”.
