-## Scene 7 — The student takeaway
-<!-- claim_id: 01-c6 -->
+### Scene 7 — Your acceptance criterion stays central
+<!-- 01-c7 -->
 
 **Narration**
 
-Our practical interpretation is to test a bounded loop with an inspectable history and explicit validation gates. NanoProteinLM reports that four of seven changes survived its final ablation study. Selection needs testing too. The supplied evidence comes from author-run studies and a first-party research blog; no independent replication or outside researcher assessment is supplied. Distinct reporting teams are not independent replications of one result. More agents, or more recorded activity, should not be assumed to increase discovery efficiency.
+For students, the defensible delegation unit is a bounded change with a fixed evaluator and an explicit scientific acceptance criterion. Successful execution alone should not count as completing the intended claim. Across these reports, no independent replication is supplied. They establish neither a universal success rate nor a student-scale cost advantage. Use them to design an inspectable experiment, while retaining responsibility for deciding whether its evidence answers your question.
 
 **Visual**
 
-Three cards: **Bound the experiment · Preserve the history · Validate selected changes**. Footer: **Interpretation; independent replication not supplied**.
+Return to the opening diagram. End on “Did the evidence satisfy the intended claim?” Label the recommendation **Interpretation** and the replication and cost limits **Uncertainty**. Cite [solar segmentation, §2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10491), excerpt “Failed runs are treated as rejections”; [ResearchFlow-Eval, Appendix G](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18088), “Example of reported success without a met criterion”; and [NanoProteinLM, “What survived the human gate”](https://jojoml.github.io/blog/2026/nanoproteinlm-agenticls/), “A final scaled-up ablation of the confirmed improvements.”
 
-Source locators: [NanoProteinLM](https://jojoml.github.io/blog/2026/nanoproteinlm-agenticls/), **What survived the human gate**, “Four of the seven changes survive the final ablation study.” [Malena](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40303), §4.4, base-iteration comparison. [Agora](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18094), Abstract, matched-comparison requirement. Evidence-coverage limitation refers to the supplied five-source collection.
